.TH SINTERACTIVE 1 "August 2026" "sinteractive 0.5.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sinteractive \- start an interactive tmux session on a Slurm compute node
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sinteractive
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RB [ \-\-
.IR SBATCH_ARGS... ]
.br
.B sinteractive \-\-attach
.RI [ JOBID | NAME ]
.br
.B sinteractive \-\-cancel
.IR JOBID | NAME
.br
.B sinteractive \-\-list
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B sinteractive \-\-status
.RI [ TARGET ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B sinteractive \-\-refresh
.RI [ TARGET ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B sinteractive \-\-ensure
.I NAME
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B sinteractive \-\-agent\-context
.br
.B sinteractive \-\-install\-claude
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sinteractive
submits a batch job that launches a detached
.BR tmux (1)
session on the allocated compute node, then SSHes in and attaches to
it. Because the shell lives in tmux, the session survives SSH drops and
can be reattached later with
.BR \-\-attach .
.PP
Options not listed below are passed directly to
.BR sbatch (1),
so any sbatch resource flag (e.g.
.BR \-\-gres ,
.BR \-\-exclusive ,
.BR \-\-qos )
works as expected.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Launch options
.TP
.BI \-n\fR,\ \fB\-\-name\  NAME
Tag the session with a name for easy reattach. Allowed characters:
letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
.BR \- .
The name appears in
.B squeue
(as job name
.BI sint\- NAME ),
in
.B \-\-list
output, and can be used anywhere a
.I JOBID
is accepted. Launching a second session with a name already in use is
refused.
.IP
Note that
.B \-n
is consumed by sinteractive as
.BR \-\-name ;
use
.BI \-\-ntasks= N
to set sbatch ntasks instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-node\  NODE
Request a specific compute node (alias for
.BR \-\-nodelist ),
e.g.
.BR compute01 .
.TP
.BI \-\-partition\  PART
Slurm partition (default:
.BR interactive ).
.TP
.BI \-\-time\  TIME
Wall time limit (default: 1 day). Accepts shorthand in addition to
Slurm formats; see
.B TIME FORMATS
below.
.TP
.BI \-j\fR,\ \fB\-\-threads\  N
Number of CPUs (alias for
.BR \-\-cpus\-per\-task ;
default: 2).
.TP
.BI \-m\  SIZE
Memory (alias for
.BR \-\-mem ),
e.g.
.BR 16G
(default: 8G).
.TP
.B \-\-mouse
Enable tmux mouse support: wheel scrollback, click to select panes,
drag borders to resize. Off by default because it intercepts terminal
selection; hold Shift to select text for an OS-level copy. With mouse
support on, tmux's own mouse selection copies out over SSH via OSC 52.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mouse
Disable mouse support (overrides
.BR SINTERACTIVE_MOUSE ).
.TP
.B \-\-detach
Launch without attaching: wait until the session is ready, print
connection info (attach/status/cancel commands), and return.
Attach later with
.BR \-\-attach .
Useful for scripts and agents; combine with
.B \-\-json
for machine-readable output. Unlike a normal launch,
.B \-\-detach
is allowed from inside an existing session (it never nests tmux).
.SS Session management
.TP
.BI \-a\fR,\ \fB\-\-attach\ [ TARGET ]
Reattach to a running session by
.I JOBID
or
.IR NAME .
If multiple sessions share a name, specify the job id.
.IP
With no
.IR TARGET ,
reattach to your only running session. If several are running, they are
listed with ready-to-run
.B \-\-attach
commands so you can pick one; if none are, the error says how to start
one.
.TP
.BI \-\-cancel\  TARGET
Cancel a session by
.I JOBID
or
.IR NAME ,
reporting what was cancelled. Equivalent to
.BR scancel (1)
on the job, but accepts session names. Allowed from inside a session
\(em cancelling the session you are in ends it, like the wall time
running out.
.TP
.BI \-\-ensure\  NAME
Idempotent get\-or\-create: if a session named
.I NAME
is already running, report it and exit 0 without launching anything;
otherwise launch one and report that. Implies
.BR \-\-detach ,
and takes the same launch options as a normal launch (\c
.BR \-\-time ,
.BR \-j ,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-\-partition ,
and any
.BR sbatch (1)
flag). With
.B \-\-json
the object carries an extra
.B created
field \(em
.B true
when this call launched the session,
.B false
when it found one already running \(em so a caller need not list, parse,
and recover from a duplicate\-name error.
.IP
A
.B PENDING
match counts as existing and is returned with its real state rather than
waited on. The duplicate\-name check happens before submission, so two
concurrent
.B \-\-ensure
calls for the same name can still both launch.
.TP
.BR \-l ,\  \-\-list
List running sinteractive sessions with job id, name, node, partition,
elapsed time, time limit, and the session's current working directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-status\  [TARGET]
Show session status: state, node, partition, allocated CPUs and memory,
elapsed time, and time remaining.
.I TARGET
is a
.I JOBID
or
.IR NAME ;
inside a session it defaults to the current session.
.TP
.BI \-\-refresh\  [TARGET]
Same output as
.BR \-\-status ,
and additionally asks the session to re-check its end time against Slurm
and rewrite its state file now instead of at its next poll. Use it after
changing a job's wall time with
.BR "scontrol update JobId=... TimeLimit=..." ,
so cached readers agree immediately.
.TP
.B \-\-json
With
.BR \-\-list ,
.BR \-\-status ,
or
.BR \-\-detach :
print JSON for scripts and agents.
.B \-\-status \-\-json
and
.B \-\-list \-\-json
return the same object shape, except that
.B \-\-list
additionally carries
.BR cwd ,
which costs an SSH round\-trip per session. Both include
.BR end_epoch " and " remaining_seconds ,
so one
.B \-\-list
call can rank sessions by remaining wall time, and
.BR cpus ", " memory ", " memory_mb " and " gpus ,
which describe the session's own allocation.
.IP
.B cpus
is what Slurm
.I allocated,
which can exceed what was requested \(em a cluster that hands out whole
cores will report 2 for a
.B \-j 1
session. That is the number you actually have.
.IP
Those resource fields are for sizing a
.I separate
allocation to run work in, not for sizing work run inside the session;
see
.BR \-\-agent\-context .
They are deliberately not exported into the session environment.
.TP
.B \-\-agent\-context
Print a briefing, on stdout, for a coding agent running inside a session:
which job it is in, the allocation's size, the wall time remaining, and
the rule that a session is an orchestration shell rather than a compute
target \(em with the
.BR srun (1)
and
.BR salloc (1)
command shapes to use instead. Exits 1 with a short note when run outside
a session. Intended for a
.B SessionStart
hook (see
.BR "SCRIPTING AND AGENTS" ),
but useful by hand to see exactly what an agent was told.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-quota
Re-check storage usage against the hard quota now, rewrite the shared cache,
and tell every running session to re-read it. Prints a one-line summary, or
JSON with
.BR \-\-json .
Exits 0 whether or not the user is over: being over quota is a fact to
report, not a failure of the check. Exits 1 only when the quota cannot be
read at all.
.IP
Sessions poll this on their own every
.B $SINTERACTIVE_QUOTA_POLL
seconds (default 600) and show a red
.B QUOTA
notice at the left of a second status line, below the session line, while it
holds. It reports the overage, not the usage \(em the number to act on.
Running
.B \-\-check\-quota
after freeing space clears that notice within a tick rather than at the end
of the poll interval, which is what makes it useful to an agent that has just
deleted something.
.IP
Usage comes from the quota daemons named by
.BR $SINTERACTIVE_QUOTA_HOSTS ,
on
.BR $SINTERACTIVE_QUOTA_PORT ,
and the hard limit from
.BR $SINTERACTIVE_QUOTA_FILE .
Where those are absent the notice never appears, so a cluster that does its
quotas differently is unaffected.
.TP
.B \-\-install\-claude
Install the Claude Code skills and hooks into
.I ~/.claude
(or
.BR $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR ),
then register the hooks in
.IR settings.json .
That merge is done with
.BR jq (1)
and only with it: it is additive (appended to whatever
.B .hooks
already holds), idempotent (a hook already registered in
.I settings.json
or
.IR settings.local.json ,
matched by script name, is skipped, and an unchanged result is not
written), and it keeps the file it replaces as
.IR settings.json.bak\- STAMP.
A symlinked
.I settings.json
is resolved first, so a dotfiles repo gets its target edited rather than
its link replaced, and one that does not parse is reported and left
alone. Without
.BR jq (1)
the block is printed to merge by hand instead. The assets are installed
beside the script by
.BR "make install" ,
under
.IR <prefix>/share/sinteractive ,
and are found relative to the running script, so this works from an
installed copy with no checkout present. Set
.B SINTERACTIVE_SHARE
to override the location.
.TP
.BR \-h ,\  \-\-help
Show a short help message.
.TP
.BR \-V ,\  \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH TIME FORMATS
.B \-\-time
(and
.BR \-t )
accept shorthand that is converted to Slurm's
.I [D\-]HH:MM:SS
format:
.BR 8h ,
.BR 30m ,
.BR 2d ,
.BR 1d12h ,
.BR 1h30m .
Values carry over, so
.B 90m
becomes 1:30:00 and
.B 25h
becomes 1\-01:00:00. Native Slurm formats (anything containing
.B :
or
.IR D \-)
and pure integers (minutes) pass through unchanged.
.SH DEFAULTS
With no flags, a session gets 1 day of wall time, 2 CPUs, and 8G of
memory on the
.B interactive
partition. Personal defaults can be set with
.B SINTERACTIVE_*
environment variables (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT );
explicit flags always win.
.SH WAITING FOR A JOB TO START
While the submitted job is pending, sinteractive polls the scheduler
every five seconds and reports why it is waiting \(em the Slurm pend
reason, e.g. waiting for free resources or behind higher-priority jobs
\(em together with Slurm's estimated start time, when it has one, and
how long you have been waiting. On a terminal this is a single line
that updates in place; when output is redirected (logs, scripts) a dot
is printed per poll instead.
.PP
Pressing
.B Ctrl\-C
while waiting cancels the pending job and says so.
.SH SUBMISSION CHECKS
Before submitting, sinteractive notes any sessions you already have
running and how to reattach to them, then continues \(em a reminder
aimed at the forgotten\-detach case, not a refusal.
.PP
Submission is refused, with an explanation, when:
.IP \(bu 3
the requested
.B \-\-time
would overlap an upcoming Slurm maintenance (MAINT) reservation \(em
Slurm would not start the job until after maintenance anyway. The error
suggests a shorter
.B \-\-time
that fits before the window. Passing
.B \-\-reservation
explicitly skips this check.
.IP \(bu 3
launching would exceed the interactive partition's per-user job limit.
The error lists your current sessions with reattach and cancel
commands.
.SH IN-SESSION KEYS
.TP
.B Ctrl\-b h
Open a help popup with job info (node, time remaining), detach and
reattach commands, and basic tmux keys. Any key closes it.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-b d
Detach from the session; the job keeps running. Reattach later with
.BR "sinteractive \-\-attach" .
.TP
.B Ctrl\-b $
Name (or rename) the session in place. Updates the squeue job name and
the name used by
.BR \-\-attach / \-\-status .
.TP
.B Ctrl\-b [
Enter copy mode for scrollback and keyboard selection. Copy mode uses
vi keys regardless of
.BR $EDITOR :
.B Space
starts a selection
.RB ( V
selects whole lines),
.B Enter
copies and exits, and
.B ?
searches up
.RB ( /
searches down). Copied text is pushed to the local system clipboard
over SSH via OSC 52;
.B Ctrl\-b ]
pastes into a pane. Press
.B q
to leave copy mode without copying.
.PP
Ending the shell in every pane
.RB ( exit )
ends the session and releases the allocation.
.SH STATUS BAR
The dot at the left of the tmux status bar tracks time remaining:
shown in the terminal's foreground color while more than an hour is
left (configurable via
.BR SINTERACTIVE_WARN_YELLOW ),
then pulsing yellow with a countdown, and a spinner blinking yellow/red
with an M:SS countdown for the final 10 minutes
.RB ( SINTERACTIVE_WARN_RED ).
The terminal bell rings once as the final countdown starts, so the
terminal tab is flagged even when you are looking elsewhere.
About ten seconds before the limit, a notice is printed in every pane
and the session shuts down cleanly, so the job completes instead of
being killed at timeout.
.PP
Nothing else is crowded into that line. Anything the session has to say
about itself appears below it, on status lines of its own, each present
only while it has something to say and costing a row of pane height only
while it is there. Nearest the session line are the warnings:
.B QUOTA
in red at the left of the line (see
.BR \-\-check\-quota ),
and at its right, in yellow, the end time when the request was trimmed to
finish before a maintenance reservation. Both hold for the life of the
session, so when both apply they share the line, one at each end of it \(em
and each keeps its side whether or not the other is there. Below them, furthest from the session
line because it is only an offer, is the Claude Code hint (see
.BR "CLAUDE CODE INTEGRATION" ).
.PP
The rule between the pane and the status bar carries no text at all.
.SH SCRIPTING AND AGENTS
Headless workflows (e.g. Claude Code) can launch and inspect sessions
without attaching:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sinteractive \-\-detach \-n build \-\-time=8h \-\-json
sinteractive \-\-ensure build \-\-time=8h \-\-json  # get\-or\-create
sinteractive \-\-status build \-\-json     # incl. remaining_seconds
sinteractive \-\-refresh build \-\-json    # same, after a scontrol change
.RE
.fi
.PP
Inside a session,
.B SINTERACTIVE_JOB_ID
(and
.BR SINTERACTIVE_NAME ,
if named) are exported, and
.B sinteractive \-\-status
needs no target. A state file (see
.BR FILES )
mirrors the countdown so the time budget can be polled without hitting
the scheduler. Do not trust a budget read earlier in a long-running
session: wall time can be changed underneath you with
.BR scontrol (1),
so re-read it before starting work that has to fit.
.SS A session is not a compute target
A session is an orchestration shell. It defaults to the
.B interactive
partition \(em the smallest on the cluster \(em and it is shared with the
shell the user is typing in. Run work in an allocation sized for it
instead:
.PP
.nf
.RS
srun \-p PART \-c N \-\-mem SIZE \-t TIME \-J NAME \-\-comment=NAME \-\- CMD...
salloc \-\-no\-shell \-p PART \-c N \-\-mem SIZE \-t TIME \-J NAME \-\-comment=NAME
srun \-\-overlap \-\-jobid=ID \-\- CMD...              # ... reused
scancel ID
.RE
.fi
.PP
Give every job a short descriptive name in both Slurm fields \(em
.B \-J NAME
and
.BR \-\-comment= NAME ,
the same value \(em so a shared queue says what is running and why:
.B squeue
shows the name in
.B %j
and the comment in
.BR %k .
Both belong to the allocation, so naming the
.BR salloc (1)
covers every
.B srun \-\-overlap
step run inside it.
.PP
Both stream output and propagate the command's exit code.
.BR salloc (1)
prints
.RB \(dq "Granted job allocation ID" \(dq
on standard error, not standard output, so capture the id through
.BR 2>&1 .
Because every
.B SLURM_
variable is stripped from a session (so that tools inside it do not
believe they are running as a job step),
.BR srun (1)
and
.BR salloc (1)
run from inside one create their own allocations rather than steps of the
session's job.
.SS Claude Code hooks
.B \-\-agent\-context
prints this briefing for the current session. Six skills and two hooks wire
it up \(em
.I bodhi\-compute
for cluster etiquette,
.I slurm\-discovery
for partitions, accounts and QOS,
.I bodhi\-storage
for where data belongs,
.I bodhi\-software
for modules and environments,
.I slurm\-batch
for array jobs and dependencies, and
.I git\-workflow
for the git conventions, plus one hook briefing the agent at session start
and one warning it when the session is running out of wall time \(em
installed with
.B sinteractive \-\-install\-claude
from any installed copy, or
.B make claude\-install
from a checkout. Both also register the hooks in
.I settings.json
when
.BR jq (1)
is available; see
.BR \-\-install\-claude .
.PP
While Claude Code is running in a session whose integration is not yet
registered, a yellow
.B "sinteractive \-\-install\-claude"
hint sits on the bottom status line, below any warnings \(em furthest
from the session line, because it is an offer rather than something to
act on. It is a whole line and fits an 80-column terminal, so it is
read at a glance rather than scrolled past. It appears only while a
.B claude
process is actually running, and clears once the hooks are registered, so
it never nags anyone who does not use Claude Code.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Set personal defaults, e.g. in
.IR ~/.bashrc ;
explicit flags always win.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_TIME
Default wall time (e.g. 8h, 2d). Default: 1 day.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_PARTITION
Default partition. Default: interactive.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_QOS
Default Quality of Service, passed as
.BR --qos .
Unset by default (no QOS is added); set it on schedulers that require an
explicit QOS.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_CPUS
Default CPU count. Default: 2.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_MEM
Default memory (e.g. 16G). Default: 8G.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_TMUX
Path to the
.B tmux
binary used on the compute node. Default: /usr/local/bin/tmux.
Set this when tmux lives elsewhere (e.g. /usr/bin/tmux on clusters that
ship it as a system package).
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_MOUSE
.BR on | 1 | true | yes
enables mouse support. Default: off.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_WARN_YELLOW
Seconds remaining at which the status-bar countdown turns yellow.
Default: 3600.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_WARN_RED
Seconds remaining at which the blinking final countdown starts.
Default: 600.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_GRACE
Seconds remaining at which the session shuts down cleanly.
Default: 10.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_POLL
Seconds between end-time re-checks against Slurm, which is also how often
the state file is rewritten. Default: 30. Raise it where scheduler traffic
is a concern, at the cost of a wall-time change taking that much longer to
reach the cached file.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_QUOTA_POLL
Seconds between storage-quota checks. Default: 600. The result is cached per
user rather than per session, so one poll serves every session open.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_QUOTA_FILE
Pipe-delimited file of hard quotas
.RI ( user | size | email ).
Default:
.IR /cluster/scripts/quota_current.txt .
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_QUOTA_HOSTS
Space-separated quota daemons to sum usage across. Default: the nine Bodhi
daemons on 172.20.8.110\-118.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_QUOTA_PORT
Port those daemons listen on. Default: 9878.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_QUOTA_TIMEOUT
Seconds to wait for each daemon's reply. Default: 5.
.TP
.B SINTERACTIVE_COLOR
.BR auto | always | never
for the launch and teardown narration. Default:
.BR auto ,
which colours only when standard error is a terminal and
.B TERM
is neither
.B dumb
nor empty. The test is on standard error rather than standard output because
that is where the narration goes: a redirect of stdout still narrates to your
terminal, and
.B 2>log
stays free of escapes.
.B NO_COLOR
(any value, per no\-color.org) disables colour;
.B SINTERACTIVE_COLOR=always
overrides both, for piping into something that renders escapes itself.
.PP
Exported inside a session:
.B SINTERACTIVE_JOB_ID
and, when the session is named,
.BR SINTERACTIVE_NAME .
.SH COMPLETION
A bash completion is installed by
.BR make " " install .
It completes options and, after
.BR \-\-attach ,
.BR \-\-status ,
and
.BR \-\-cancel ,
the job ids and names of running sessions. Targets come from the state
files in
.I ~/.cache/sinteractive
(see
.BR FILES )
rather than the scheduler, so completion stays instant when
.BR squeue (1)
is slow.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.cache/sinteractive/JOBID.json
Time-remaining state file, refreshed about every 30 seconds while the
session runs (see
.BR SINTERACTIVE_POLL )
and removed at teardown. Contains
.BR job_id ,
.BR name ,
.BR node ,
.BR end_epoch ,
.BR remaining_seconds ,
and
.BR updated_epoch .
.B $HOME
is shared, so it is readable from the login node too.
.IP
The end time is re-checked against Slurm immediately before every write, so
.B updated_epoch
is when the whole snapshot was confirmed: if it is more than about two
minutes old, treat the file as stale and fall back to
.BR \-\-status .
Age it exactly with
.BR "remaining_seconds \- (now \- updated_epoch)" .
A wall-time change made with
.B scontrol update JobId=... TimeLimit=...
appears within one poll interval, or at once with
.BR \-\-refresh .
When
.BR squeue (1)
cannot be reached the file is left untouched rather than restamped, so it
ages honestly instead of vouching for a budget nobody verified; the
status-bar countdown keeps running from the last known end time.
.TP
.I ~/.claude
Written by
.BR \-\-install\-claude :
the skills under
.IR skills/ ,
the hooks under
.IR hooks/ ,
and their registration merged into
.IR settings.json ,
whose previous version is kept as
.IR settings.json.bak\- STAMP.
Set
.B $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
to use a different directory.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B sinteractive
1-day session, 2 CPUs, 8G memory.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-n myproj \-\-time=8h
Named 8-hour session.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-node compute01
Run on a specific node.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-time=2h \-p rna
2-hour session on the rna partition.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-mem=32G \-j 8
32G of memory and 8 CPUs.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-attach myproj
Reconnect after a disconnect or detach.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-attach
Reconnect to your only running session.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-cancel myproj
Cancel a session by name.
.TP
.B sinteractive \-\-detach \-n build \-\-time=8h
Headless launch for scripts and agents.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; nonzero if submission is refused, a target session
cannot be found or is ambiguous, or the session fails to start.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sbatch (1),
.BR srun (1),
.BR squeue (1),
.BR scancel (1),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR jq (1)
.PP
Bodhi cluster documentation:
.UR https://rnabioco.github.io/bodhi\-docs/sinteractive/
.UE
.SH HISTORY
A clean-room reimplementation, inspired by the original sinteractive by
P\[:a]r Andersson (NSC, Sweden) and the later CU Boulder adaptation by
Jonathon Anderson. Maintained by Jay Hesselberth.
